Contemplating Contemplation

For most of human history, the illuminated ones, who have borne many titles, have rediscovered the practice of contemplation. We are all familiar with it on some level. We stare out a window with a question hanging somewhere behind the eyes, turn an experience over while walking, pray on something, pull an old book from the shelf because a sentence we once passed over suddenly seems to matter. Sometimes a question follows us for years before we begin to understand what it even meant.

So in this primer on contemplation I would like to present a compressed version of what contemplation actually is, so we can become more deliberate about developing the faculty without strangling it under technique or suffocating beneath thousands of years of dogma. This is meant to be a seed from which someone in the far-flung future could rediscover mysteries buried in looming towers of text. Let me invite you on a personal journey of contemplating contemplation.

The human being is a layered organism that we can broadly divide into three domains: the will of the body, the will of the nervous system, and the will of the rational mind. Each carries its own priorities and influence over us to varying degrees, and in classic Hermetic recursive fashion their relationship shapes our behavior and the world as it appears from inside. Thoughts alter the interior landscape over time while that landscape continually influences which thoughts are likely to arise in the future. A memory can tighten the chest before the rational mind has even finished remembering, or an imagined future can alter the breath while nothing whatsoever has happened outside the imagination.

Because thought has this potency, meditation gives us a way to cultivate the interior landscape so thoughts can become orderly and clear. Through meditation we gain greater control over attention and create space for thoughts to breathe, and really that is one of the central principles of contemplation itself: creating enough space for thought to take lucid form. Once that space exists, instead of immediately being carried away by whatever appears, we can begin studying it. Contemplation grows from gathering the scattered pieces of experience, discovering relationships between them, slowly building our own internal models of the world and allowing those models to change as we do.

The written word, though sacred, is not the Word itself. Written truth remains a representation of something that ultimately has to be apprehended. You can memorize a thousand pages about forgiveness and still discover, when the knife actually goes in, that you have no idea what forgiveness means. Study gives us inherited maps. Contemplation makes us walk the country, and once you have walked some of it yourself the maps become far more interesting.

One of the oldest ways of approaching this territory might be called stalking the self. To stalk yourself, cultivate what I call a zero attitude. Put the mind in neutral and assume a receptive internal state, something like the quiet observation a hunter brings to an unfamiliar animal whose behavior he genuinely wants to understand. Carry some of the neutral emotional posture developed through meditation into ordinary life and watch what happens before the rational mind starts cleaning up the evidence. A person says something and your stomach tightens. You suddenly want to leave the room. Some absurdly small task develops the emotional gravity of an approaching execution. Pay attention to the sequence while it is still fresh. The explanation can come later.

If you immediately condemn the reaction, defend it, or construct a philosophy around it, you have already disturbed what you were trying to observe. Give the animal enough room to behave naturally. There is also a recursive turn hidden inside the practice: every once in a while, while observing what is happening, notice that you are the one observing it. Anger may occupy most of the room and yet some faculty is still capable of seeing anger happen. Attention can become so identified with whatever captures it that we disappear into the experience and forget we are present at all.

Once you begin watching this way, certain questions naturally start presenting themselves. Let curiosity guide what you explore, but let immediacy constrain what deserves your contemplation. There are vast questions common to human life, but lived reality usually gives you a perfectly good place to begin. Information gathered randomly tends to fragment and muddy the mind; you can collect facts for years and end up with an attic full of interesting boxes and no idea where the screwdriver is. Knowledge gathered around a living question already has somewhere to attach itself.

Formal study can provide structure through pedagogy, but direct experience can organize knowledge even more deeply because the information becomes entangled with emotion, consequence, memory and narrative. I have sometimes imagined this as a library somewhere in the liminal space between wakefulness and dreams, each room tying knowledge and experience together into a larger conceptual frame. Whether that library is anything more than imagination does not matter much to me. The useful part is learning to trust that knowledge has relationships and giving the mind enough context to find them.

The same thing happens when we turn observation inward. A journal can let experiences that occurred weeks apart sit beside each other long enough for relationships to become visible that otherwise would have been brushed off as business as usual. Putting something on paper or speaking it aloud changes how it is held in the mind. It creates an imprint of a perspective generated inside a particular internal state, preserving enough of the configuration that memory, emotion, sensation and thought can later be examined together.

And very often the things that feel obvious to us, the things we are certain we already understand, are exactly where the blind spots live. Simple concepts can sit so far upstream that a whole rat's nest of assumptions has grown beneath them without ever being examined. Catch a charged moment while it is still fresh and record what you can see from inside it: the narrative, the concepts around it, the feelings in the body, whatever strange association appears. Return a few days later when the charge has shifted. Something that appeared perfectly obvious Tuesday afternoon may look very strange Friday morning. Sit with that difference without immediately deciding which version of you was correct.

As observations accumulate, a deeper geometry begins showing itself. Information and thought have geometry. Every concept has elements, configurations and constraints, nested inside other conceptual structures whose relevance shifts with context. Relationships begin forming patterns larger than any individual piece, which is why sometimes the mind recognizes a fit before it can explain why.

Thoth taught us to be light, to listen to intuition but weigh feeling against reason. Precise language becomes powerful here because a poorly understood word, or even a poorly worded question, can conceal several different realities beneath one symbol.

For the purpose of demonstration, I want to talk about a time in my life when contemplating forgiveness helped me survive.

Many years ago I had a difficult experience with a family member that caused me great material and spiritual harm. There was, and perhaps always will be, a part of me that wanted justice. My rational mind could project possible scenarios far into the future and construct very persuasive arguments for why punishment of the person who harmed me was necessary. The social landscape at the time made pursuing justice a zero-sum game. The sacrifices required would have damaged relationships I cared about and threatened my connection to my family, so I was forced to play the long game.

Hatred and anger are heavy things to carry, especially when the darker reaches of human nature begin entertaining homicidal revenge. That charge has to live somewhere while you are carrying it, and mine found plenty of places to live in rumination and despair. So I began praying and asking God for wisdom, love and his divine peace. I surrendered the problem to the divine and asked for guidance toward some way of forgiving the person who had wronged me.

The problem was that I did not actually know what forgiveness meant.

So forgiveness became a question I carried with me, and that gradually became a process of passive contemplation. The practice itself is simple: keep a concept you genuinely want to understand somewhere in the back of the mind and allow life to accumulate around it. I did not sit there continuously thinking about forgiveness. I let it follow me. Scripture opened one angle, my observations and internal dialogue opened others, other people's relationships became evidence, and the question remained alive without demanding an immediate answer.

For a long time I could say, rationally and sincerely, that I had forgiven the person.

In my body and nervous system it was not even close.

That contradiction bothered me because I had treated forgiveness as though it were one thing. I had made a rational decision and somewhere underneath it my body was apparently refusing to obey.

At one point, while thinking about the person who had attacked me, I had a flash in my mind of the other people he had affected throughout his life, their angry ghosts following behind him. Something became clear to me in that image. I began to understand the rage I was carrying as something that had entered me through contact with another person's internal state. The resentment did not feel native anymore. I had collided violently with someone carrying his own emotional charge and had walked away carrying some of it inside myself.

However anyone else wants to explain that experience is their business. For me it created a distinction I could actually use. I was not responsible for another person's feelings, baggage or lack of self-control, and I could put down what had been handed to me. Not because the person deserved freedom from my judgment or contempt, but because I did not deserve to carry him through everything. I did not want to be haunted by that dark thoughtform of unquenchable rage.

I came to think of this as release. The other person did not have to cooperate. It was something that could happen inside me while I still understood exactly what they had done and structured my life accordingly. Forgiveness, I was discovering, did not require me to hand someone the knife again.

But I still had family ties around the situation, and for their sake I continued trying to repair the relationship. Perhaps there was a dangerous and foolish degree of self-erasure in that choice; I can see pieces of that now that I could not see while I was inside it. Still, remaining in contact long enough taught me something that the rational decision to forgive never could: trust was a different animal.

My nervous system did not care that I had reached an elegant conclusion about forgiveness. It learned from repeated experience. If this person behaved differently over time, some of the activation diminished. If the old pattern returned, the body remembered immediately. I began to think of this middle territory as forbearance: a willingness to allow some form of peace and continued relationship while the injury remained unresolved, giving another person room to demonstrate something different without pretending the original pattern had never happened.

That experience also forced me to notice how dangerous it can be to project our own psychology onto another person. Human beings share plenty of standard-issue machinery, but people can organize that machinery into radically different mindsets. Some people will use an opening in a way you never would because they are not standing where you are standing. Zero attitude has to apply there too. Observe the person who is actually in front of you rather than the imaginary version built out of yourself.

And eventually I discovered that another process had been hiding beneath the word forgiveness: exoneration.

I was never able to exonerate my adversary. I had been attacked, driven into the streets, alienated from my family and humiliated, and the injury moved in slow motion stretched across decades. Exoneration could not occur entirely inside my own mind because it required something from the other person. When a similar situation arose again, they would have to respond differently because they recognized that they had fucked up, found the upstream problem inside themselves and gone to work unfucking it. Not merely regret the consequence, not say the correct words, but become different enough that the change was demonstrated when reality gave them another opportunity to make the same mistake.

That is rare because it is extremely difficult, but contemplating it turned the question back on me as well. My own blunders became harder to regard merely as humiliations to survive. They were places where reality had caught me doing something incorrectly, which made them unusually rich material if I could bear the discomfort long enough to figure out what upstream part of myself had generated them.

My situation with this person did not end in exoneration. Predictably, after about fifteen years the whole thing crashed and burned, and the people who had wronged me eventually stepped on the rake they had left out.

The contemplation still did what I needed it to do.

What changed was the internal structure of the problem. Rational forgiveness, release from resentment, bodily trust and actual repair of the relationship had all been stacked beneath one word, and I had been demanding that every layer of myself obey the same decision at the same speed.

One word had concealed several different realities.

That kind of discovery is one reason contemplation eventually has to reach beyond our own immediate experience. Other people have already spent forty years thinking about questions that may have entered our head last Tuesday, and sometimes wisdom consists of having enough humility to go find out what they discovered. Scripture is central to this in my own practice, but so are old masters, philosophy, history, biography, literature and the accumulated record of human beings trying to understand the same strange world.

This is where another mode of contemplation begins to emerge: immersion. I like to approach a thinker through the world that produced the thought. Historical narrative gives the information a trunk. Once you understand something of the world in which an idea arose, details stop floating around as isolated facts and begin attaching themselves to people, causes, consequences, rivalries, disasters, friendships, pressures and everything else that gave the thought its shape. You can begin inhabiting the world imaginatively enough to ask what reality looked like from where that person was standing and what problem they thought they were trying to solve.

Sit with someone long enough, even across centuries, and the marble bust starts becoming human. Their ideas regain emotional context and so do their errors. You can see where a profound insight may have grown alongside some ridiculous assumption inherited from the time and place in which they lived, which should make us slightly suspicious of our own ability to see beyond the water we happen to be swimming in.

Then turn the same studious receptivity back upon yourself.

Your personal history is also a story, and that story remains active in the present. An event becomes an interpretation; the interpretation gets repeated; eventually it begins predicting what kind of person you are and what kind of world you inhabit. Somewhere along the way this happened to me can quietly become this is who I am. Self-importance can enter through the same machinery. Injured pride may become an elaborate theory of justice. The need to be right can dress itself in excellent logic and stroll straight through the front door. Zero attitude allows you to notice this without turning self-observation into self-prosecution, and when the prosecutor inevitably appears, notice him too. Scrutinize his case.

This becomes more difficult when you encounter parts of yourself that you would rather call evil, broken, diseased, pathetic or simply not-you. Those parts can appear in the mind like apparitions, especially when they have been pushed far enough away from ordinary consciousness that you no longer understand what purpose they are serving. Sometimes the conflict announces itself through dreams, young wizard, dramatized wars playing out at night between aspects of yourself that have not figured out how to live in the same kingdom.

So when approaching those parts of yourself, shut the fuck up and listen.

The zero attitude still applies. Instead of immediately trying to cut out whatever frightened or disgusted you, inhabit it for a moment. Let it write. Feel what the world looks like from where it is standing. What does it want? What does it think it is protecting? What catastrophe does it imagine will occur if it stops doing whatever ridiculous thing it has been doing?

Somewhere around here contemplation naturally begins bleeding into the larger problem of integration. I have come to think of integration partly as a negotiation within the self, and any negotiation becomes impossible when one side is not allowed to speak. If there is an internal war going on, the rational mind does not get to announce itself king, declare victory and wonder why the countryside keeps catching fire. But integration belongs to the larger work. For contemplation the important thing is learning how to hear what was previously buried beneath noise, judgment and explanation.

Externalizing what you find helps because material that remains recursive inside the mind can be very difficult to examine from another angle. Writing does this directly. An emotionally charged idea can circulate between memory and sensation until repetition itself begins feeling like evidence. Put it on a page and now the thing has to sit still. Return tomorrow and what felt like revelation at midnight may look like it was written by a raccoon trapped in a liquor cabinet.

Serious conversation can do something similar because your private model encounters another mind. I have also found AI useful for this in a way I did not expect. I can externalize an internal state while I am still inside it, before memory cleans up the edges, and then return later to observations that might otherwise have vanished back into the stream. I do not need the machine to tell me what anything ultimately means. Often its value is simply that the conversation gives thought somewhere outside my own head to accumulate.

Art goes somewhere stranger. Creative play can loosen the usual arrangement of mind enough for something to appear before the rational voice knows exactly what it is saying. You finish a song or poem and notice information inside it you never consciously decided to put there. Sometimes the thing making the art knew what you were contemplating before the part of you that speaks in declarative sentences caught up.

When enough of these traces accumulate across time, another property of thought becomes visible: trajectory. An interior movement goes somewhere, and the emotional tone at the beginning does not necessarily tell you where it will end. Something that initially feels peaceful can slowly tighten the whole interior world around itself, while a painful thought may unfold into clarity after the first resistance passes. For me this is inseparable from prayer. I ask God for wisdom because the rational mind is extraordinarily capable of constructing explanations for things some deeper part of me has already decided it wants, and sometimes the only way I notice the deception is by watching what kind of interior world grows around the thought over time.

Trajectory also reveals when thought has stopped moving at all. A question that was once opening begins circling the same ground. Memory feeds emotion, emotion summons the memory back, and the rational mind arrives with another stack of arguments for why it needs to go around one more time. Sometimes the driver is obvious pain. Sometimes thinking itself creates a feeling of safety or control, so the mind keeps building because building feels preferable to whatever would happen if it stopped.

This is rumination, and there can be enormous intellectual activity inside it while almost nothing new is being learned.

When I notice myself trapped there, meditation becomes useful again. Leave language alone for a while. Take a deep breath and put attention somewhere simpler: breath, bodily sensation, prayer, mantra, silence. The unresolved question can remain quietly bookmarked while some of the charge attenuates. Nothing says contemplation requires continuous conscious thought. Sometimes the missing piece simply has not arrived yet, and life keeps happening while you are doing something else until some observation falls into place three days later and changes the geometry.

That is passive contemplation again, except now you understand why the passivity matters. The question is alive without being strangled for an answer.

There is another way contemplation can curl inward on itself because thinking is pleasurable. A clever rational mind can spend forty minutes constructing a metaphysical cathedral around the fact that somebody hurt its feelings. The cathedral may even contain some excellent architecture, but if the original object has stopped changing while language continues multiplying, eventually the pleasure of thought itself becomes part of what needs to be stalked.

Abstraction gives this enormous room to happen. As an idea moves farther from material reality, reality loses some of its ability to correct us immediately and interpretation gains freedom. More of the thinker begins leaking into the thought unnoticed: ego, fear, personal history, the need for recognition, even assumptions absorbed from the collective consciousness of the culture and historical moment in which the thinker happens to live. Intelligence does not necessarily solve this problem. A powerful rational mind simply has more equipment available for constructing a persuasive defense of something another part of the person already wanted to believe.

This is why I try to ground contemplation in base reality whenever reality is capable of answering. It is also why the ability to suspend belief matters. The more abstract the subject becomes, the more deliberately I have to include my own relationship to the idea among the things being contemplated. If contradiction begins feeling personally threatening, that is information. If my identity has fused with a conclusion, that is information. If I find myself rebuilding an entire metaphysics because one ugly little fact will not fit through the front door, that is probably information too.

At some point you have to let the world retain the right to surprise you.

And then keep looking.
